.Dd September 24, 2026
.Dt PW 8
.Os NextBSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm pw
.Nd account and group maintenance
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Cm useradd | userdel | usermod | usershow | usernext
.Op Ar name
.Op Ar options
.Nm
.Cm groupadd | groupdel | groupmod | groupshow | groupnext
.Op Ar name
.Op Ar options
.Nm
.Cm lock | unlock
.Ar name
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
creates, edits and removes accounts and groups.
NextBSD keeps regular accounts in the DirectoryServices property lists and
system accounts in
.Pa /etc/master.passwd ,
and
.Nm
works on both.
.Pp
.Sy Which store an operation uses is decided for you .
An account that already exists is edited wherever it lives.
A new one is placed by its shape, which is the only part worth learning:
.Bl -bullet -offset indent
.It
A uid or gid of 999 or below is a system account.
.It
A name beginning with an underscore is a system account.
.It
A shell of
.Pa nologin
with no home, or a home of
.Pa /nonexistent ,
is a system account.
.It
Anything else is a directory account.
.El
.Pp
Everything belonging in
.Pa /etc/master.passwd
is handed, with the command line untouched, to the
.Nm
.Fx
ships, which is vendored into this program rather than run as a second binary.
So a system account is managed by exactly the code that has always managed it,
which is how packages that create daemon users keep working.
.Ss Why the threshold is 999
The ports collection registers 647 users, and 267 of them sit between 500 and
999.
Most would be caught by the shell-and-home rule, but around twenty would not:
.Li postgres
at 770,
.Li jenkins
at 818 and
.Li ejabberd
at 543 all have a real shell and a real home.
A lower threshold would file those in the property lists, give them a computed
home that is not where their data lives, and hide them from
.Xr pwd_mkdb 8 .
.Pp
That leaves 1000 to 5000 unallocated, and NextBSD's own users start at 5001
with the
.Li admin
group at 5000.
.Sh WHAT A DIRECTORY ACCOUNT CANNOT HAVE
These are refused rather than quietly ignored, because a flag that appears to
work and does nothing is worse than one that fails:
.Bl -tag -width "-l newname" -compact
.It Fl L
a login class.
The property lists have no such field.
.It Fl d
a home directory.
It is always
.Pa /Local/Users/ Ns Ar name ,
computed from the name.
.It Fl l
renaming.
The record is keyed by name.
.El
.Pp
All three are accepted normally for a system account, since the base copy
handles those and understands them.
.Sh SUBCOMMANDS
.Bl -tag -width "groupshow" -compact
.It Cm useradd
Create an account.
With no
.Fl u
the first free uid at or above 5001 is used.
A new directory account is created locked, with no usable password; set one
with
.Xr passwd 1 .
.It Cm userdel
Remove an account.
.Fl r
removes the home directory as well.
.Xr rmuser 8
is the fuller tool, and also removes group memberships, the crontab and any
running processes.
.It Cm usermod
Change an account.
.It Cm usershow
Print an account in
.Xr passwd 5
form, or a readable form with
.Fl P .
The exit status says whether it exists, which is what scripts use it for.
.It Cm groupadd , groupdel , groupmod , groupshow
The same for groups.
.Cm groupmod Fl m
adds members and
.Fl d
removes them.
.It Cm lock , unlock
Disable and re-enable an account by marking its stored hash.
The same marker
.Xr passwd 1
uses, so the two agree.
.It Cm usernext , groupnext
Report the next free id.
Handled by the base copy, since a caller asking for an id is about to create a
system account.
.El
.Pp
.Cm "pw useradd"
and
.Cm "pw user add"
are both accepted.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "/Local/Library/DirectoryServices/Users.plist" -compact
.It Pa /Local/Library/DirectoryServices/Users.plist
.It Pa /Local/Library/DirectoryServices/Groups.plist
.It Pa /Local/Library/DirectoryServices/Domain.plist
present when this machine is the directory server, in which case
.Nm
writes here and never consults
.Pa /Network
.It Pa /Network/Library/DirectoryServices/Domain.plist
present when this machine is bound to a server, in which case
.Nm
refuses to write
.It Pa /Local/Library/DirectoryServices/Binding.plist
names that server for the message; it does not decide the role
.It Pa /etc/master.passwd
where a system account is filed
.It Pa /etc/pw.conf
read by the vendored half, as it always was
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
Values from
.In sysexits.h :
.Ev EX_USAGE
for a flag that cannot be represented,
.Ev EX_NOPERM
for a non-root caller or a machine joined to a directory server,
.Ev EX_NOUSER
for a name that does not exist,
.Ev EX_UNAVAILABLE
when the base copy is missing,
.Ev EX_DATAERR
for a property list that will not parse, and
.Ev EX_IOERR
for a failed write.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Create a regular account and give it a password:
.Pp
.Dl # pw useradd joe
.Dl # passwd joe
.Pp
Create a daemon account, which goes to
.Pa /etc/master.passwd
without being asked to:
.Pp
.Dl # pw useradd _builder -u 300 -g 300 -d /nonexistent -s /usr/sbin/nologin
.Pp
Add somebody to a group:
.Pp
.Dl # pw groupmod staff -m joe
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr chpass 1 ,
.Xr passwd 1 ,
.Xr adduser 8 ,
.Xr rmuser 8 ,
.Xr vipw 8
.Sh HISTORY
This
.Nm
was written for NextBSD.
It keeps the command line of the tool it replaces, because package install
scripts depend on it, and adds the routing between the two stores that this
system needs.
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Joseph Maloney
